    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}=$",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot (-1) & 1\\cdot (-2) & 1\\cdot 6 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot (-1) & (-6)\\cdot (-2) & (-6)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-1) & 1\\cdot (-2) & 1\\cdot 6 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (-1) & (-2)\\cdot (-2) & (-2)\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -36 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
